"""Scoped existing buyer-readiness refresh, not a new acquisition intent."""
from refresh_dscr_purchase_methods import refresh

BODY='''<p class="lead">Before buying an STR, distinguish a cleaner's phone number from accepted backup capacity for this address. A usable arrangement identifies who can accept the work, the covered dates, complete price, access, linen responsibility and the person who approves readiness. The purchase question is whether the buyer can open with a funded, tested handoff—not how many names appear in the seller's contact list.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the cleaner acceptance register and launch cash plan to an acquisition call</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its contracted acquisition scope; this worksheet does not promise vendor availability or include cleaning in a service agreement.</p>
<h2>Verify the buyer's coverage, not the seller's relationship</h2>
<p>Ask the seller or manager for authorized introductions, the actual property checklist, linen inventory and existing scope. Obtain the proposed provider's separate acceptance for the new buyer and intended opening dates. The seller's past use does not establish assignment of an agreement, transfer of prepaid balances or capacity during your launch window. Have counsel review continuing obligations and the insurer review applicable coverage questions; this guide does not certify worker classification or insurance.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/2895">Airbnb's current ground rules for home hosts</a> require clean listings before check-in and cleaning between stays. That platform standard explains why readiness matters; it is not a cleaner certification, an availability promise or a substitute for the property's detailed checklist. A listing's cleaning charge does not prove the buyer has already paid the provider or will recover every backup cost.</p>
<p>Keep the scope narrow. The <a href="/blog/str-property-management-fees/">management-fee review</a> handles the overall agreement, while <a href="/blog/after-hours-guest-support-cost/">after-hours support</a> addresses remote response and local incident dispatch. A cleaner who accepts a turnover has not necessarily accepted guest communication, repairs, emergency response or a decision to refund a stay.</p>
<h2>Complete an address-specific acceptance register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Obtain before relying on capacity</th><th>Buyer decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Property and service</td><td>Address, spaces, beds, tasks, exclusions and accepted checklist version</td><td>Does the quote cover the actual furnished home rather than a generic bedroom count?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Window and activation</td><td>Covered dates, notice needed, acceptance method and unavailable periods</td><td>Who confirms this specific job, and what happens if nobody accepts?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complete price</td><td>Turn rate, rush/holiday charges, laundry, supplies, minimums and payment dates</td><td>Which amounts replace normal turnover cost and which are additional?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retainer or reservation payment</td><td>What is reserved, included jobs, credits, expiration and cancellation terms</td><td>Does payment buy capacity, only a discount, or neither?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access and materials</td><td>Authorized entry, code duration, linens, keys, supply location and replacement responsibility</td><td>Can the accepted team complete the agreed work without the seller's private credentials?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inspection and escalation</td><td>Completion evidence, independent approver, rework scope and spending authority</td><td>Who decides the home is ready, and who funds unresolved work?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>A tentative introduction remains a lead. Record unanswered fields as unresolved rather than assigning a zero cost or claiming guaranteed same-day service. Shared laundry, transport or staffing can leave two providers dependent on the same bottleneck. Ask how the proposed arrangement handles that actual dependency; two company names alone do not establish independent capacity.</p>
<h2>Use a paid trial as evidence, not as a guarantee</h2>
<p>Arrange an authorized paid trial or shadow turnover before opening when transaction permissions allow. Agree on price and scope first. Obtain seller consent for pre-closing access, protect occupied stays and private guest information, and avoid surprise entry or a fabricated emergency. If a trial cannot occur before closing, identify the remaining test, its funded date and who must approve opening afterward.</p>
<p>Record start and completion times, missing materials, checklist exceptions, rework and the named inspector's acceptance. Test entry using appropriately authorized access, not copied seller passwords. Confirm how codes are revoked or changed after work. A successful trial establishes what happened on that date; it does not prove future holiday availability, every team's performance or compliance with every safety requirement.</p>
<p>Write a failed-trial branch: who can correct the work, what it costs, how readiness is rechecked and whether opening must be delayed. The cleaner should escalate hazards to the authorized owner or appropriate professional, not perform work outside accepted competence. Do not promise a platform refund, seller reimbursement or insurance payment to fund the correction.</p>
<h2>Worked quote comparison: same nominal cost can buy different coverage</h2>
<p>All figures are invented, not vendor quotes, market benchmarks or client results. Assume two proposals for identical stated turnover tasks and a covered rush activation. Proposal A has no monthly retainer, a $240 turnover and a $60 rush charge: $300 per accepted activation. Proposal B has a $120 monthly retainer, a $200 turnover and a $40 rush charge: $120 plus $240 per accepted activation. Assume the retainer is not credited to jobs and both proposals exclude the same other services.</p>
<p>At two accepted rush activations in one month, A costs $600 and B costs $600. At four, A costs $1,200 and B costs $1,080. With no activations, A costs zero under these assumptions and B still costs $120. This arithmetic does not establish equal availability, equal quality or a preferred vendor. If B's retainer only buys a price discount, do not describe it as reserved capacity; if A has no accepted window, its low idle cost does not solve launch readiness.</p>
<p>Recalculate actual terms for an ordinary activation, credited jobs, a different month or excluded laundry. Do not multiply one busy month into a forecast. Separately compare accepted service and inspection rights before selecting a price; an unsupported offer cannot become usable coverage through favorable arithmetic.</p>
<h2>Bridge only incremental backup costs into purchase cash</h2>
<p>Separate hypothetical launch example using proposal A, not a continuation of the quote comparison's workload. Assume $280,000 spendable acquisition capital and $262,000 already allocated to down payment, closing, setup and a protected $40,000 reserve. The allocation also includes two ordinary $180 turnovers in the launch period. The unassigned margin is $18,000; the protected reserve is inside the $262,000 and is not another $40,000 available to spend.</p>
<p>Assume an additional $300 paid trial, $600 of distinct replacement linens and a $900 nonrefundable pre-opening reservation payment that buys a separately accepted window but credits no jobs. These three costs total $1,800. Allocation rises to $263,800 and unassigned margin falls to $16,200. The hypothetical $900 payment is not proposal B's monthly retainer; obtain actual capacity terms rather than infer them from the amount.</p>
<p>If the two already-budgeted ordinary turnovers are replaced by two accepted $300 backup turns, the extra cost is two times ($300 minus $180), or $240—not another $600 on top of the original $360 allowance. Allocation becomes $264,040, leaving $15,960. If a distinct $500 opening-delay bill then becomes payable, allocation is $264,540 and margin $15,460. No lost-booking revenue or automatic reimbursement is modeled. Replace these assumptions with dated bills and actual receipts; an annual profit projection cannot pay an earlier trial invoice.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">purchase-budget worksheet</a> to place each payment before its due date. Confirm that replacement linens are not already funded elsewhere and that the seller's prepaid balance is not assumed available without documented closing treatment. Expected bookings and hoped-for tax refunds are not cleared launch funds.</p>
<h2>Make readiness part of the acquisition decision</h2>
<p>Proceed when the actual coverage, material supply, test and inspection plan fit the buyer's intended opening and cash limits. Reprice a still-viable acquisition when verified incremental costs change the budget. Delay opening or seek a valid transaction extension when a decisive acceptance or trial remains unresolved; counsel determines contractual rights. Decline a plan that requires unaccepted capacity or unavailable cash to work. Never infer guaranteed launch readiness merely because the seller has hosted before.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Discuss the property shortlist with a bounded readiness memo</a>: accepted provider, remaining tests, opening decision-maker, dated cost and fallback. The <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income purchase roadmap</a> connects these checks to capital and diligence. Share only authorized, necessary records through agreed secure channels; do not publish access codes, guest details or vendor insurance files.</p>
<p class="small">Airbnb primary policy reviewed October 7, 2026; policies and actual provider terms may change. BNB Accelerator is the publisher and an interested acquisition service provider; this is a document-based buyer worksheet, not firsthand vendor testing. Educational only, not legal, insurance, tax or personalized investment advice. All amounts are hypothetical. No availability, opening date, reimbursement, tax benefit or investment result is guaranteed.</p>'''
FAQ=[
('Does a seller\'s backup cleaner automatically transfer to the buyer?', 'No transfer is assumed. Obtain the provider\'s acceptance for the buyer, address, dates, scope, prices and access. Review assignment and prepaid balances under the actual documents.'),
('Does paying a retainer guarantee backup availability?', 'Not by itself. Verify whether it reserves accepted capacity, includes work, credits invoices or only buys a discount. Confirm exclusions, unavailable dates, activation and cancellation terms.'),
('Should I add the full backup invoice to an existing cleaning allowance?', 'Reconcile the scope first. If backup work replaces an already-budgeted ordinary turn, add only the incremental difference. Distinct trial, reservation and linen costs need separate lines without double counting.'),
('What does a successful paid trial prove?', 'It documents completion and inspection on that date under the agreed scope. It does not guarantee future capacity or universal safety compliance. Keep an accepted activation and failed-readiness plan.')]
if __name__=='__main__':
 refresh('backup-cleaner-str','What Makes an STR Backup Cleaner Real | BNB Accelerator','What Makes an STR Backup Cleaner Real?','Before buying an STR, verify accepted backup-cleaner capacity, test the handoff and fund incremental launch costs. Use a coverage register and cash worksheet.',BODY,FAQ,'2026-09-23','data-backup-cleaner-purchase-link','Verify <a href="/blog/backup-cleaner-str/">accepted backup-cleaner capacity and the incremental launch-cash bridge</a>. A seller contact list or paid retainer is not proof the buyer can open on schedule.')
